 "speed": 1.0,
 "text": "What if a model could answer without writing a word? A chat model replies one token at a time, and your code has to parse whatever comes back. A decision model works differently. You give it the state, like a support ticket, and typed questions with the answers you allow. It reads everything once, and returns a probability for every option. There are three kinds of question. Choice picks one label. Noul is a yes or no, given as a probability. Score places the input on a scale. And it can't answer outside the options you gave it. TypeSafe launched Jev, a hosted model, in September. Cloudflare followed with open-weight Clef and Clef Flash, and Ollama now runs them on your own machine. We use Clef Flash as a judge. It looks at frames from our videos, and answers every rubric question in a single call. It won't explain itself, and hard reasoning still needs a big model. So let the decision model decide, and let the language model write.",
